meta {
  name: Update Policy
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/policies/{{soldPolicyId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "carrier": "Allstate Northbrook",
    "premium": 1275.50,
    "items": 3,
    "expirationDate": "2027-02-01",
    "status": "Active"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the saved policy in the Lead Detail shape", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("id");
    expect(body).to.have.property("policyType");
    expect(body).to.have.property("carrier");
    expect(body).to.have.property("policyNumber");
    expect(body).to.have.property("active");
    expect(body).to.have.property("status");
    expect(body).to.have.property("premium");
    expect(body).to.have.property("items");
    expect(body).to.have.property("effectiveDate");
    expect(body).to.have.property("expirationDate");
  });

  test("applies exactly what was sent", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.carrier).to.equal("Allstate Northbrook");
    expect(body.premium).to.equal(1275.5);
    expect(body.items).to.equal(3);
    expect(body.status).to.equal("Active");
  });

  test("leaves omitted fields alone", function () {
    // `policyNumber` and `policyType` were not in the body; the sale's values
    // must survive. This is what makes it a patch rather than a replace.
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.policyNumber).to.equal(bru.getVar("soldPolicyNumber"));
    expect(body.policyType).to.equal("Auto");
  });

  test("returns calendar dates, not instants", function () {
    // A full ISO timestamp is how an effective date becomes the previous day
    // for a client west of Greenwich.
    expect(res.getBody().expirationDate).to.equal("2027-02-01");
    expect(res.getBody().effectiveDate).to.equal("2026-02-01");
  });

  test("exposes only display fields (no internals leak)", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("agencyId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("branchId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("policyNumberKey");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("legacySmartSuiteId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("isTestRecord");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /policies/:id — sold-policy quick edit (PAC-56 #27)

  Backs the **Sold card** on the Lead Detail page. David asked for that card to
  allow "quick edits to sold policies"; legacy had this as a whole second
  Fillout form (the Deals `Edit Sold` formula → form `782Dc42qhSus`).

  This is a **field correction, not a re-submission**. `POST /sold-deals`
  remains the only thing that creates deals, policies and the audit items a sale
  generates — re-running the wizard to fix a transposed policy number would
  duplicate the service hand-off.

  - **Module:** `deal_audits` (must be enabled for the agency)
  - **Permission:** `deal_audits:write`
  - **Data scope:** clamped on the policy's **deal**, 404 (never 403) outside it

  > **Why `deal_audits`?** It inherits the Sold write path's gate: correcting
  > what that form wrote is the same act as writing it, and the roles that can
  > record a sale (Producer, Branch Manager, CRM, Agency Owner) are exactly the
  > ones that should be able to fix a typo in it.
  >
  > ⚠ Consequence: disabling the `deal_audits` module also disables this edit.

  ## Body — every field optional, object must not be empty
  | field          | type            | rules                                            |
  |----------------|-----------------|--------------------------------------------------|
  | policyNumber   | string \| null  | ≤ 60 chars; re-normalized into `policyNumberKey`  |
  | policyType     | enum            | a canonical `POLICY_TYPES` label                  |
  | carrier        | string \| null  | ≤ 120 chars                                       |
  | premium        | number          | 0 – 1,000,000                                     |
  | items          | integer         | 0 – 100                                           |
  | effectiveDate  | string \| null  | `YYYY-MM-DD` — a calendar date, not an instant     |
  | expirationDate | string \| null  | `YYYY-MM-DD`                                      |
  | status         | string \| null  | free text; there is no canonical status vocabulary |

  `null` **clears** an optional field; omitting it leaves the stored value
  alone. The two are different requests — a producer blanking a mistyped carrier
  is not one who only touched the premium.

  ## Deliberately not editable
  - **`active`** — `Household.totalActivePolicies` is a stored roll-up nothing
    recomputes on write, so flipping `active` here would drift a household's own
    count away from its policies. Retiring a policy is a lifecycle event, not a
    typo fix.
  - **`householdId` / `dealId`** — re-parenting a policy is a merge, and would
    move a record between producers' books.

  ## No duplicate check
  `PolicySchema` is **non-unique on purpose**: migrated data already holds
  duplicate numbers, and different carriers legitimately reuse them. Warn-and
  -link (`GET /policies/check` + `DuplicatePolicyNotice`) belongs on the create
  path, where the producer is choosing between "link" and "correct". Blocking a
  typo fix because the corrected number already exists would strand the record.

  ⚠ **Do not add a unique index** — it would fail to build on migrated data.

  ## Policy-number format (PAC-56 #20)
  If the request **changes `policyNumber`** and the effective carrier (the
  patched value if present, the stored one otherwise) carries a
  `policyNumberPattern` from `GET /carriers`, the new number must satisfy it —
  tested against the normalized key, so punctuation is fine.

  ⚠ Checked **only when the number changes**. Validating on a carrier-only edit
  would strand every migrated policy whose stored number predates the rule: a
  rule introduced today must not retroactively lock records written before it.

  ## Deal roll-ups ARE recomputed (PAC-56 #25)
  `Deal.premium` / `itemCount` / `policyCount` / `policyTypes` / `isBundle` /
  `dealType` are recomputed from the deal's stored policies after the save. Until
  this landed they were left alone, so the Sold card's footer total could
  disagree with the row directly above it.

  **`soldDate` / `soldDateYmd` are never recomputed.** `soldDateYmd` is the Sold
  scorecard's indexed bucket key; a Thursday correction must not re-date a
  Monday sale.

  ⚠ **This moves reported numbers.** The Sold scorecard sums `deals.premium` and
  the leaderboard ranks on it, so a premium correction here changes a producer's
  dashboard figure and possibly their rank. Intended — a scorecard built on
  numbers known to be wrong is worse — but say so when demoing it.

  ⚠ **Migrated deals are skipped**, gated on `premiumSource === 'snapshot'` (the
  value `ResolveDealStep` stamps on deals this app created). A migrated deal's
  premium is SmartSuite's rollup over rows we may hold only part of, so
  recomputing would overwrite a historical figure with the imported subset. The
  trade: a typo fix on a migrated deal will not move its total.

  The recompute is best-effort and post-save — the correction is what the
  producer asked for, and failing the request after it committed would report
  the wrong outcome.

  ## Response `200`
  The saved policy, in the same shape `GET /leads/:id` returns for
  `household.policies[]` and `deal.policies[]` — one mapper
  (`policies/policy-view.ts`) serves both, so the client swaps the row in place
  rather than refetching the whole 360° assembly.

  ```json
  {
    "id": "665f...ac1",
    "policyType": "Auto",
    "carrier": "Allstate Northbrook",
    "policyNumber": "BRU-1770000000000",
    "active": true,
    "status": "Active",
    "premium": 1275.5,
    "items": 3,
    "effectiveDate": "2026-02-01",
    "expirationDate": "2027-02-01"
  }
  ```

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — empty body, an unknown `policyType`, a malformed date,
    or a number out of range.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `deal_audits` module disabled, or missing `deal_audits:write`.
  - `404 Not Found` — unknown/malformed id, **or** a policy outside the caller's
    data scope. Both are the same answer on purpose. A policy with no deal
    cannot be attributed and is therefore a 404 under `own` scope.

  ## Every edit is logged (PAC-65 #9)

  This is the only way to correct a booked sale — `POST /sold-deals` is
  create-only and there is no deal PATCH — so it is the "sold" half of the
  quote/sold edit log. A patch that changes at least one of the eight fields
  writes a `field_changed` activity carrying `{ field, label, kind, from, to }`
  per change, with `policyId` recording **which** policy of the deal was touched.

  Reading the log needs `agency:changelogs:read` (Agency Owner and Branch
  Manager). This collection logs in as a producer, so the row will not surface
  in `GET /leads/:id` here.

  Code-backed fields are stored **normalized** (`carrier`, `policyType`): a
  migrated policy holds raw SmartSuite select values, and nothing re-normalizes a
  change row on read.

  ⚠ The row is written even when the policy cannot be traced to a lead — no
  `dealId`, or a migrated deal with no `leadId`. Such a row carries `dealId` and
  `policyId` but no `leadId`, so it appears on **no** timeline today; it is
  recorded anyway rather than letting the audit trail silently omit edits to the
  oldest records.

  ## Why this lives in `Sold Deals/` and not `Policies/`
  Same reason as `Check Policy Number (Match)`: Bruno walks folders
  alphabetically, so `Policies/` runs **before** any sale exists and there is no
  policy to patch. The endpoint itself is on `PoliciesController`.
}
